"mt_9VZem2vUqk":{"intro":"Different seasons, different stars! 🌌","items":[{"t":"pick","q":"Why do we see different constellations in different seasons?","opts":["Earth's orbit changes which way we face at night","Stars move away in winter","Clouds hide them"],"why":"At night we look away from the Sun, and that direction changes over the year."},{"t":"pick","q":"Which constellation, with a three-star 'belt', is easy to see on winter evenings in the US?","opts":["Orion","Scorpius","Cygnus"],"why":"Orion the Hunter rules the winter sky."},{"t":"pick","q":"Which constellation is a summer evening sight in the US?","opts":["Scorpius","Orion","Taurus"],"why":"Scorpius shows low in the south in summer."},{"t":"tf","q":"The Big Dipper can be seen all year from most of the US.","a":true,"why":"It circles near the North Star and never fully sets for much of the US."},{"t":"tf","q":"Stars rearrange their patterns each season.","a":false,"why":"The patterns stay the same; our view changes."}]},
