"mt_UjuriPLVgT":{"intro":"Hunting for planets around other stars! 🔭🪐","items":[{"t":"match","q":"Match each method to how it works.","pairs":[["transit","a planet dims its star's light as it passes"],["radial velocity","a star wobbles from a planet's tug"],["direct imaging","photographing the planet itself"]]},{"t":"pick","q":"What is the habitable zone?","opts":["Where liquid water could exist on a planet","Right next to the star","The center of a galaxy"],"why":"Not too hot, not too cold — the 'Goldilocks zone'."},{"t":"pick","q":"An exoplanet is…","opts":["a planet orbiting another star","a moon","a comet"],"why":"Exo means outside our solar system."},{"t":"pick","q":"Which gas in an atmosphere could hint at life?","opts":["Oxygen mixed with methane","Pure helium","No gas at all"],"why":"Such combinations could be biosignatures."},{"t":"tf","q":"In the radial velocity method, the star's light shifts color as it wobbles toward and away from us.","a":true,"why":"That's the Doppler effect."}]},
